Chapter 1. Admin Guide

1.1. Overview

   This module ships structured log entries from the Kamailio
   configuration script to a log server over HTTP. The entries are
   collected in batches and posted. The format of the request is selected
   with the format parameter, the supported formats are described in the
   section Formats.

   Logging is asynchronous: the SIP worker processes only pack the values
   of the log entry and pass them to a dedicated sender process. The
   sender process serializes the entries to JSON, collects them in a queue
   and posts them in batches, so the SIP processing is never blocked by
   the HTTP transfer.

   The set of fields of a log entry is described by log groups. A log
   group is defined with the default_map and map parameters and is
   referenced by its key as the first argument of the http_log() function.
   Fields of the default map are filled automatically (current time, route
   name, pseudo-variables, static values), fields of the map are filled
   from the arguments of http_log().

   Several targets (log server instances) can be configured for high
   availability. Batches are sent to the targets in round-robin order;
   when a request fails, the batch is retried on the next target.

1.2. Dependencies

1.2.1. Kamailio Modules

   The following modules must be loaded before this module:
     * pv - needed when pseudo-variables are used in default_map or in the
       text arguments of http_log(), which is the case in any practical
       configuration.

1.2.2. External Libraries or Applications

   The following libraries or applications must be installed before
   running Kamailio with this module loaded:
     * libcurl.

   The module is available only on Linux: the sender process is built on
   epoll and timerfd.

1.3. Parameters

1.3.1. target (str)

   URL of the ingestion endpoint of the log server, see the section
   Formats for the endpoint of each format. The parameter can be set
   multiple times to configure several targets, they are used in
   round-robin order with failover to the next target when a request
   fails.

   Default value is empty (at least one target is required).

   Example 1.1. Set target parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "target", "http://127.0.0.1:9200/_bulk")
modparam("log_http", "target", "http://10.0.0.2:9200/_bulk")
...

1.3.2. format (str)

   Format of the request and its options, in the form
   name[=>option=value[;option=value...]]. The name is one of
   elasticsearch or ndjson, the options are specific to the format and are
   described in the section Formats. A value containing commas or spaces
   has to be enclosed in single or double quotes. An unknown option is a
   configuration error. The parameter can be set only once, a second
   definition is a configuration error.

   Default value is "elasticsearch" (no options).

   Example 1.2. Set format parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "format", "elasticsearch=>index=kamailio-%Y.%m.%d")
...
modparam("log_http", "format", "ndjson")
...

1.3.3. auth_username (str)

   User name for HTTP basic authentication on the targets. The
   authentication is enabled when both auth_username and auth_password are
   set.

   Default value is empty (no authentication).

   Example 1.3. Set auth_username parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "auth_username", "kamailio")
...

1.3.4. auth_password (str)

   Password for HTTP basic authentication on the targets. See
   auth_username.

   Default value is empty (no authentication).

   Example 1.4. Set auth_password parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "auth_password", "secret")
...

1.3.5. timeout (int)

   Maximum duration of one HTTP request in milliseconds. A request that
   takes longer is treated as failed and the batch is retried on the next
   target.

   Default value is 5000.

   Example 1.5. Set timeout parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "timeout", 2000)
...

1.3.6. chunked (int)

   If set to 1, the request body is sent with chunked transfer encoding:
   the entries of a batch are serialized one by one while curl is sending,
   so only one entry at a time is kept in the request buffer. If set to 0,
   the whole batch is assembled in memory first and sent with a
   Content-Length header, for servers or proxies that do not accept
   chunked requests.

   Default value is 1.

   Example 1.6. Set chunked parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "chunked", 0)
...

1.3.7. batch_size (int)

   Maximum number of log entries in one HTTP request. When the number of
   queued entries exceeds this value, a request is sent immediately
   without waiting for batch_delay.

   Default value is 0 (no limit, the whole queue is sent in one request).

   Example 1.7. Set batch_size parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "batch_size", 100)
...

1.3.8. batch_delay (int)

   Interval in milliseconds between the sending of the queued entries. The
   minimum value is 100.

   Default value is 0 (100 milliseconds).

   Example 1.8. Set batch_delay parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "batch_delay", 1000)
...

1.3.9. queue_size (int)

   Maximum number of log entries kept in the queue of the sender process.
   When the limit is exceeded (e.g. all targets are down), new entries are
   dropped, an error is logged and the log_http_drop_total statistic is
   incremented.

   Default value is 0 (unlimited).

   Example 1.9. Set queue_size parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "queue_size", 10000)
...

1.3.10. default_map (str)

   Fields of a log group that are filled automatically on every http_log()
   call. The format of the value is described in the section Format of
   default_map and map parameters. The parameter can be set multiple
   times, once per log group.

   Default value is empty.

   Example 1.10. Set default_map parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "default_map", "gen(@timestamp:timestamp, route:route_name,
 src_ip:$si, app:&kamailio)")
...

1.3.11. map (str)

   Fields of a log group that are filled from the arguments of the
   http_log() function. The format of the value is described in the
   section Format of default_map and map parameters. The parameter can be
   set multiple times, once per log group.

   Default value is empty (at least one map is required).

   Example 1.11. Set map parameter
...
modparam("log_http", "map", "gen(message:text, code:num)")
...

1.4. Formats

1.4.1. elasticsearch

   Elasticsearch Bulk API, described in the Elasticsearch documentation:
   https://www.elastic.co/docs/api/doc/elasticsearch/operation/operation-b
   ulk.

   Options:
     * index - name of the index to put the entries in. The name may
       contain strftime() conversion specifiers, evaluated in UTC when a
       batch is sent, e.g. kamailio-%Y.%m.%d for daily indices. When not
       set, the index has to be part of the target URL.

   Example 1.12. elasticsearch format
...
modparam("log_http", "target", "http://127.0.0.1:9200/_bulk")
modparam("log_http", "format", "elasticsearch=>index=kamailio-%Y.%m.%d")
...

1.4.2. ndjson

   Newline delimited JSON: the body of the request is the JSON objects of
   the entries, one per line, with no envelope. See
   https://github.com/ndjson/ndjson-spec. The format is accepted by the
   log servers consuming a stream of JSON lines.

   The format has no options.

   Example 1.13. ndjson format
...
modparam("log_http", "target", "http://127.0.0.1:9428/insert/jsonline?_stream_fi
elds=app,group&_msg_field=message&_time_field=@timestamp")
modparam("log_http", "format", "ndjson")
...

1.5. Format of default_map and map parameters

   The value of both parameters has the format key(name:type[, name:type
   ...]).
     * key - name of the log group, used as the first argument of
       http_log().
     * name - name of the JSON field in the log entry. Special field names
       of the log server (@timestamp, _msg, _time) can be used directly.
     * type - type of the field, see below.

   Types allowed in default_map:
     * timestamp - current time in RFC3339 format with millisecond
       precision (UTC), e.g. 2026-10-06T08:09:41.123Z.
     * route_name - name of the route block from which http_log() is
       called.
     * $... - a pseudo-variable, its value is taken at the time of the
       call (e.g. $si, $ci, $var(x)). Null values result in the field
       being omitted.
     * &value - a static string value.

   Types allowed in map:
     * text - string. The argument may contain pseudo-variables, they are
       evaluated at the time of the call. Empty strings are omitted from
       the entry.
     * num - integer number.
     * float - floating point number.
     * bool - boolean, the argument must be true, false or a number
       (non-zero is true).
     * inet - IPv4 or IPv6 address, stored as a string.
     * date - date and time in local time zone in the format %Y.%m.%d
       %H:%M:%S, stored in RFC3339 format (UTC).

1.6. Functions

1.6.1.  http_log(key[, value1, ...])

   Build a log entry of the log group key and pass it to the sender
   process. The number of values must be equal to the number of fields in
   the map of the group, and each value must match the type of the
   corresponding field. Up to 5 values can be given.

   Returns 1 on success and -1 on error (unknown group, wrong number or
   type of values, sender process not reachable).

   This function can be used from ANY_ROUTE.

   Example 1.14. http_log usage with Elasticsearch
...
modparam("log_http", "target", "http://127.0.0.1:9200/_bulk")
modparam("log_http", "format", "elasticsearch=>index=kamailio-%Y.%m.%d")
modparam("log_http", "default_map", "gen(@timestamp:timestamp, app:&kamailio, gr
oup:&gen, route:route_name, callid:$ci, src_ip:$si)")
modparam("log_http", "map", "gen(message:text, code:num)")
modparam("log_http", "default_map", "auth(@timestamp:timestamp, app:&kamailio, g
roup:&auth, callid:$ci)")
modparam("log_http", "map", "auth(message:text, user:text, ok:bool)")
...
request_route {
    ...
    http_log("gen", "request $rm from $si", "100");
    ...
    if (!auth_check("$fd", "subscriber", "1")) {
        http_log("auth", "authentication failed", "$au", "false");
        auth_challenge("$fd", "1");
        exit;
    }
    http_log("auth", "authenticated", "$au", "true");
    ...
}
...

   Example 1.15. http_log usage with ndjson format
...
modparam("log_http", "target", "http://127.0.0.1:9428/insert/jsonline?_stream_fi
elds=app,group")
modparam("log_http", "format", "ndjson")
modparam("log_http", "default_map", "gen(_time:timestamp, app:&kamailio, group:&
gen, route:route_name, callid:$ci, src_ip:$si)")
modparam("log_http", "map", "gen(_msg:text, code:num)")
...
request_route {
    ...
    http_log("gen", "request $rm from $si", "100");
    ...
}
...

1.7. Statistics

1.7.1. log_http_queue_size

   Current number of log entries waiting in the sender queue.

1.7.2. log_http_drop_total

   Total number of log entries dropped because the queue was full.

1.7.3. log_http_err_total

   Total number of failed HTTP requests.

1.8. Queue algorithm

   The sender process collects the log entries in a queue. Every
   batch_delay milliseconds, or as soon as the queue grows over batch_size
   entries, the first batch_size entries of the queue (the whole queue
   when batch_size is 0) are posted to the current target in one request.
   Only one request is in flight at a time.

   On success (HTTP 2xx) the entries are removed from the queue and the
   next batch is sent immediately if the queue is not empty. On failure
   the log_http_err_total statistic is incremented and the same batch is
   retried on the next target. When all the targets failed, the entries
   stay in the queue until the next batch_delay tick.

   When the queue holds more than queue_size entries, new entries are
   dropped and log_http_drop_total is incremented.
